<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.8.3: Using Diagrams to Find Equivalent Quadratic Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01871</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.8.3: Using Diagrams to Find Equivalent Quadratic Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a860ef95-560f-4bfa-a75a-245dabb2c12d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>

      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">Here is a diagram of a rectangle with side lengths <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. Use this diagram to show
            that <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> are equivalent
            expressions.</para>
          <para id="para-00002">
            <media alt="Large rectangle, divided into 4 smaller rectangles. Left rectangle on top labeled x, Right rectangle on top labeled 3. Top rectangle on left side labeled x, bottom rectangle on left side labeled 1.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_06_01.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para>
          <para id="para-00003">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
            button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005">
            <media alt="Area model multiplying x+3 and x+1">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_06_02.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para>
          <para id="para-00006"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00009"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00013"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00017"><m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00007">Adding these together gives <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>


      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">Draw diagrams to help you write an equivalent expression for each of
            the following:</para>
          <para id="para-00021"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00010">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
            button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00012">The diagram should show partial products that add up to <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>25</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>


      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00026"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00014">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
            button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00016">The diagram should show partial products that add up to <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>8</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>


      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00029"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00018">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
            button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00019">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00020">The diagram should show partial products that add up to <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>3</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>


      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00030"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>m</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00022">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
            button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00023">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00024">The diagram should show partial products that add up to <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>m</m:mi>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> or <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>m</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mi>m</m:mi>
                <m:mi>n</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>


      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00025">Write an equivalent expression for each expression without drawing a
            diagram. Use the distributive property to help. Remember FOIL for multiplying binomials:
            Multiply the First terms, the Outer terms, the Inner terms, and the Last terms.</para>
          <para id="para-00033"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00027">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
            button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00028">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00061"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>8</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>12</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>


      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00062"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00031">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
            button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00032">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00063"><m:math>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>20</m:mn>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                <m:mn>50</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Using Diagrams to Find Equivalent Quadratic Expressions</title>
      <para id="para-00034">Watch the following video to learn more about using diagrams to find
        equivalent quadratic expressions.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4d74c6bb24c118c9ee654b330391716aa3d0144f"/>
      </media>
    </section>
    <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you
    will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->

    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00004">
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
        <!--Start
        interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all
        have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the
        button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00008">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00035">Image 1</para>
            <para id="para-00036">
              <media alt="Square, side length = x. 5 rectangles, width = x, length = 1. 6 squares with side length = 1.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_06_03.svg"/>
              </media>

            </para>
            <para id="para-00037">Image 2</para>
            <para id="para-00038">
              <media alt="Rectangle decomposed into smaller squares and rectangles">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_06_04.svg"/>
              </media>

            </para>
            <para id="para-00039">Are Image 1 and Image 2 equivalent? Explain or show your
              reasoning.</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00040">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00041">Yes, because <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mi>·</m:mi>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
        interaction-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00009">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00042">What does this tell you about an equivalent expression for <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>6</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>?</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00043">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00044">This shows that an equivalent expression is <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
        interaction-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00010">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            <para id="para-00045">Is there a different non-zero number of 1-by-1 squares that we
              could use instead that would allow us to arrange the combined figures into a single
              large rectangle?</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00046">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00047">We could use four 1-by-1 squares. This would give the expression <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>. The rectangle
              would be <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>. <media alt="">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_06_05.jpg"/>
              </media></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End
        interaction-->
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00012">
        <problem id="prob-00012">
          <para id="para-00048"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_7_8_3_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
    </note>

    <section id="sect-00006">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Multiplying Binomials</title>
        <para id="para-00049">Write an expression equivalent to <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00050"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Distribute. Use FOIL. Multiply the First
          terms, Outer terms, Inner terms, and Last terms.</para>
        <para id="para-00051">
          <media alt="DIAGRAM SHOWING X PLUS 2 TIMES X PLUS 5. ON THE TOP, IN RED, AN ARROW IS GOING FROM X TO X. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM X TO 5. ON THE BOTTOM, IN BLUE, AN ARROW IS GOING FROM 2 TO X. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM 2 TO 5.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_08_01.jpg" width="100"/>
          </media>

        </para>
        <para id="para-00052"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Write out the four terms.</para><para id="para-00064"><m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>5</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>10</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00053"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms.</para><para id="para-00065"><m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>7</m:mn>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>10</m:mn>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math></para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Multiplying Binomials</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00011">
            <problem id="prob-00011">
              <para id="para-00054">Write an expression equivalent to <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
          .</para>
              <para id="para-00055">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis>
          button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00011">
              <para id="para-00056">Here is how to multiply binomial expressions:</para>
              <para id="para-00057"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Distribute.</para>
              <para id="para-00058">
                <media alt="DIAGRAM SHOWING X PLUS 7 TIMES X PLUS 3. ON THE TOP, IN RED, AN ARROW IS GOING FROM X TO X. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM X TO 3. ON THE BOTTOM, IN BLUE, AN ARROW IS GOING FROM 7 TO X. ANOTHER ARROW IS GOING FROM 7 TO 3.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_10_08_02.jpg"/>
                </media>

              </para>
              <para id="para-00059"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Write out the four terms. <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>7</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00060"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms. <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>21</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math></para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>